      "n_counts": "landmark studies",
      "verbatim": "Fifty-three papers were deemed ‘landmark’ studies (see ‘Reproducibility of research findings’). It was acknowledged from the outset that some of the data might not hold up, because papers were deliberately selected that described something completely new, such as fresh approaches to targeting cancers or alternative clinical uses for existing therapeutics. Nevertheless, scientific findings were confirmed in only 6 (11%) cases."
    },
    {
      "statistic": "validation rate",
      "value": 25.0,
      "unit": "percent",
      "measures": "share of published preclinical studies that could be validated to the point at which projects could continue",
      "compared": "published preclinical studies vs validation attempts",
      "species": [],
      "n": null,
      "n_counts": "published preclinical studies",
      "verbatim": "A team at Bayer HealthCare in Germany last year reported4 that only about 25% of published preclinical studies could be validated to the point at which projects could continue."
    }
  ],
  "headline_index": 0,
  "what_the_study_did": "The authors attempted to reproduce findings from landmark preclinical cancer studies and reviewed industry validation rates of preclinical studies.",
  "what_it_found": "Only 11% of landmark preclinical cancer studies could be confirmed, and only about 25% of published preclinical studies could be validated by another company.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Many landmark findings in preclinical oncology research are not reproducible, in part because of inadequate cell lines and animal models.",
      "topic": "Reproducibility Issue"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Nevertheless, scientific findings were confirmed in only 6 (11%) cases.",
      "topic": "Confirmation Rate"
    },
    {
      "quote": "A team at Bayer HealthCare in Germany last year reported4 that only about 25% of published preclinical studies could be validated to the point at which projects could continue.",
      "topic": "Industry Validation"
    },
    {
      "quote": "The inability of industry and clinical trials to validate results from the majority of publications on potential therapeutic targets suggests a general, systemic problem.",
      "topic": "Systemic Problem"
    },
    {
      "quote": "The lack of rigour that currently exists around generation and analysis of preclinical data is reminiscent of the situation in clinical research about 50 years ago.",
      "topic": "Lack of Rigor"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Improving preclinical cancer research to the point at which it is reproducible and translatable to clinical-trial success will be an extraordinarily difficult challenge.",
      "topic": "Future Challenge"
    }
  ]
}
